#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:3
msgid "Dependencies Management in Setuptools"
msgstr "Setuptools 中的依賴管理"

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:5
msgid ""
"There are three types of dependency styles offered by setuptools: 1) build "
"system requirement, 2) required dependency and 3) optional dependency."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:9
msgid ""
"Each dependency, regardless of type, needs to be specified according to :pep:"
"`508` and :pep:`440`. This allows adding version :pep:`range restrictions "
"<440#version-specifiers>` and :ref:`environment markers <environment-"
"markers>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:18
msgid "Build system requirement"
msgstr "建置系統要求"

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:20
msgid ""
"After organizing all the scripts and files and getting ready for packaging, "
"there needs to be a way to specify what programs and libraries (build "
"backend) are actually needed to build the package for distribution. For "
"Setuptools, the requisite library is ``setuptools``. Specify the build "
"backend in a ``pyproject.toml`` file (see also :doc:`/userguide/quickstart` "
"or :doc:`/build_meta`):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:27
msgid ""
"[build-system]\n"
"requires = [\"setuptools\"]\n"
"#..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:33
msgid ""
"Also include any other ``setuptools`` plugins (e.g., :pypi:"
"`setuptools_scm`, :pypi:`setuptools-golang`, :pypi:`setuptools-rust`) or "
"build-time dependency (e.g., :pypi:`Cython`, :pypi:`cppy`, :pypi:`pybind11`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:37
msgid ""
"[build-system]\n"
"requires = [\"setuptools\", \"cython\", \"setuptools_scm\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:43
msgid ""
"If the project depends on a feature introduced in a specific version of "
"Setuptools, it is good practice to specify it as a lower bound:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:46
msgid ""
"[build-system]\n"
"requires = [\"setuptools >= 61.2\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:51
msgid ""
"Some may be tempted to also include an upper-bound for yet unreleased major "
"versions (e.g. ``setuptools <= 70``) or pin to a specific version (e.g. "
"``setuptools == 70.0.4``) in order to avoid the project being uninstallable "
"should those backward-incompatible changes affect this release of the "
"project. Setuptools maintainers recommend strongly against this "
"precautionary approach. The team primarily maintains one release, the latest "
"monotonically-increasing release, and encourages users to use that latest "
"release (work at HEAD). As a result, the team is cognizant of and takes "
"responsibility for making backward-incompatible changes and aims to mitigate "
"the impact of any breaking changes prior to releasing that change. By "
"pinning against an unreleased version, it causes toil (maintenance burden) "
"for each and every project that does the pinning (and the consumers that use "
"it) and increases the risk of erosion if maintenance is unsustained. This "
"tradeoff between reproducibility and compatibility is especially stark "
"because Setuptools frequently releases backward-incompatible releases for a "
"variety of reasons, many of which won't affect a given project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:69
msgid ""
"In previous versions of ``setuptools``, the ``setup_requires`` keyword "
"performed a similar function but is now considered deprecated in favor of "
"the :pep:`517` style described above. To peek into how this legacy keyword "
"is used, consult our :doc:`guide on deprecated practice (WIP) </deprecated/"
"index>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:79
msgid "Declaring required dependency"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:80
msgid ""
"This is where a package declares its core dependencies, without which it "
"won't be able to run. ``setuptools`` supports automatically downloading and "
"installing these dependencies when the package is installed. Although there "
"is more finesse to it, let's start with a simple example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:87
msgid ""
"[project]\n"
"# ...\n"
"dependencies = [\n"
"    \"docutils\",\n"
"    \"BazSpam == 1.1\",\n"
"]\n"
"# ..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:99
msgid ""
"[options]\n"
"#...\n"
"install_requires =\n"
"    docutils\n"
"    BazSpam ==1.1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:109
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"    ...,\n"
"    install_requires=[\n"
"        'docutils',\n"
"        'BazSpam ==1.1',\n"
"    ],\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:120
msgid ""
"When your project is installed (e.g., using :pypi:`pip`), all of the "
"dependencies not already installed will be located (via `PyPI`_), "
"downloaded, built (if necessary), and installed and 2) Any scripts in your "
"project will be installed with wrappers that verify the availability of the "
"specified dependencies at runtime."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:129
msgid "Platform specific dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:130
msgid ""
"Setuptools offers the capability to evaluate certain conditions before "
"blindly installing everything listed in ``install_requires``. This is great "
"for platform specific dependencies. For example, the ``enum`` package was "
"added in Python 3.4, therefore, package that depends on it can elect to "
"install it only when the Python version is older than 3.4. To accomplish this"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:138
msgid ""
"[project]\n"
"# ...\n"
"dependencies = [\n"
"    \"enum34; python_version<'3.4'\",\n"
"]\n"
"# ..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:149
msgid ""
"[options]\n"
"#...\n"
"install_requires =\n"
"    enum34;python_version<'3.4'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:158
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"    ...,\n"
"    install_requires=[\n"
"        \"enum34;python_version<'3.4'\",\n"
"    ],\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:167
msgid ""
"Similarly, if you also wish to declare ``pywin32`` with a minimal version of "
"1.0 and only install it if the user is using a Windows operating system:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:172
msgid ""
"[project]\n"
"# ...\n"
"dependencies = [\n"
"    \"enum34; python_version<'3.4'\",\n"
"    \"pywin32 >= 1.0; platform_system=='Windows'\",\n"
"]\n"
"# ..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:184
msgid ""
"[options]\n"
"#...\n"
"install_requires =\n"
"    enum34;python_version<'3.4'\n"
"    pywin32 >= 1.0;platform_system=='Windows'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:194
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"    ...,\n"
"    install_requires=[\n"
"        \"enum34;python_version<'3.4'\",\n"
"        \"pywin32 >= 1.0;platform_system=='Windows'\",\n"
"    ],\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:204
msgid ""
"The environmental markers that may be used for testing platform types are "
"detailed in :pep:`508`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:208
msgid ""
"Alternatively, a :ref:`backend wrapper <backend-wrapper>` can be used for "
"specific use cases where environment markers aren't sufficient."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:213
msgid "Direct URL dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:216
msgid ""
"`PyPI`_ and other standards-conformant package indices **do not** accept "
"packages that declare dependencies using direct URLs. ``pip`` will accept "
"them when installing packages from the local filesystem or from another URL, "
"however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:221
msgid ""
"Dependencies that are not available on a package index but can be downloaded "
"elsewhere in the form of a source repository or archive may be specified "
"using a variant of :pep:`PEP 440's direct references <440#direct-"
"references>`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:227
msgid ""
"[project]\n"
"# ...\n"
"dependencies = [\n"
"    \"Package-A @ git+https://example.net/package-a.git@main\",\n"
"    \"Package-B @ https://example.net/archives/package-b.whl\",\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:238
msgid ""
"[options]\n"
"#...\n"
"install_requires =\n"
"    Package-A @ git+https://example.net/package-a.git@main\n"
"    Package-B @ https://example.net/archives/package-b.whl"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:248
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"    install_requires=[\n"
"       \"Package-A @ git+https://example.net/package-a.git@main\",\n"
"       \"Package-B @ https://example.net/archives/package-b.whl\",\n"
"    ],\n"
"    ...,\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:258
msgid ""
"For source repository URLs, a list of supported protocols and VCS-specific "
"features such as selecting certain branches or tags can be found in pip's "
"documentation on `VCS support <https://pip.pypa.io/en/latest/topics/vcs-"
"support/>`_. Supported formats for archive URLs are sdists and wheels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:265
msgid "Optional dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:266
msgid ""
"Setuptools allows you to declare dependencies that are not installed by "
"default. This effectively means that you can create a \"variant\" of your "
"package with a set of extra functionalities."
msgstr ""
"Setuptools 允許您宣告默認不會安裝的依賴。這實際上意味著您可以為您的軟體包建立"
"一個具有一組額外功能的「變體」。"

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:270
msgid ""
"For example, let's consider a ``Package-A`` that offers optional PDF support "
"and requires two other dependencies for it to work:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:275
msgid ""
"[project]\n"
"name = \"Package-A\"\n"
"# ...\n"
"[project.optional-dependencies]\n"
"PDF = [\"ReportLab>=1.2\", \"RXP\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:285
msgid ""
"[metadata]\n"
"name = Package-A\n"
"\n"
"[options.extras_require]\n"
"PDF =\n"
"    ReportLab>=1.2\n"
"    RXP"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:298
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"    name=\"Package-A\",\n"
"    ...,\n"
"    extras_require={\n"
"        \"PDF\": [\"ReportLab>=1.2\", \"RXP\"],\n"
"    },\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:311
msgid ""
"It is also convenient to declare optional requirements for ancillary tasks "
"such as running tests and or building docs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:314
msgid ""
"The name ``PDF`` is an arbitrary :pep:`identifier <685>` of such a list of "
"dependencies, to which other components can refer and have them installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:317
msgid ""
"A use case for this approach is that other package can use this \"extra\" "
"for their own dependencies. For example, if ``Package-B`` needs ``Package-"
"A`` with PDF support installed, it might declare the dependency like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:323
msgid ""
"[project]\n"
"name = \"Package-B\"\n"
"# ...\n"
"dependencies = [\n"
"    \"Package-A[PDF]\"\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:334
msgid ""
"[metadata]\n"
"name = Package-B\n"
"#...\n"
"\n"
"[options]\n"
"#...\n"
"install_requires =\n"
"    Package-A[PDF]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:347
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"    name=\"Package-B\",\n"
"    install_requires=[\"Package-A[PDF]\"],\n"
"    ...,\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:355
msgid ""
"This will cause ``ReportLab`` to be installed along with ``Package-A``, if "
"``Package-B`` is installed -- even if ``Package-A`` was already installed.  "
"In this way, a project can encapsulate groups of optional \"downstream "
"dependencies\" under a feature name, so that packages that depend on it "
"don't have to know what the downstream dependencies are.  If a later version "
"of ``Package-A`` builds in PDF support and no longer needs ``ReportLab``, or "
"if it ends up needing other dependencies besides ``ReportLab`` in order to "
"provide PDF support, ``Package-B``'s setup information does not need to "
"change, but the right packages will still be installed if needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:365
msgid ""
"Best practice: if a project ends up no longer needing any other packages to "
"support a feature, it should keep an empty requirements list for that "
"feature in its ``extras_require`` argument, so that packages depending on "
"that feature don't break (due to an invalid feature name)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:371
msgid ""
"Historically ``setuptools`` also used to support extra dependencies in "
"console scripts, for example:"
msgstr ""
"從歷史上來看，``setuptools`` 也曾支援在主控台腳本中使用額外依賴，例如："

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:376
msgid ""
"[metadata]\n"
"name = Package-A\n"
"#...\n"
"\n"
"[options]\n"
"#...\n"
"entry_points=\n"
"    [console_scripts]\n"
"    rst2pdf = project_a.tools.pdfgen [PDF]\n"
"    rst2html = project_a.tools.htmlgen"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:391
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"    name=\"Package-A\",\n"
"    ...,\n"
"    entry_points={\n"
"        \"console_scripts\": [\n"
"            \"rst2pdf = project_a.tools.pdfgen [PDF]\",\n"
"            \"rst2html = project_a.tools.htmlgen\",\n"
"        ],\n"
"    },\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:404
msgid ""
"This syntax indicates that the entry point (in this case a console script) "
"is only valid when the PDF extra is installed. It is up to the installer to "
"determine how to handle the situation where PDF was not indicated (e.g., "
"omit the console script, provide a warning when attempting to load the entry "
"point, assume the extras are present and let the implementation fail later)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:411
msgid ""
"**However**, ``pip`` and other tools might not support this use case for "
"extra dependencies, therefore this practice is considered **deprecated**. "
"See :doc:`PyPUG:specifications/entry-points`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:417
msgid "Python requirement"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:418
msgid ""
"In some cases, you might need to specify the minimum required python "
"version. This can be configured as shown in the example below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:423
msgid ""
"[project]\n"
"name = \"Package-B\"\n"
"requires-python = \">=3.6\"\n"
"# ..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:432
msgid ""
"[metadata]\n"
"name = Package-B\n"
"#...\n"
"\n"
"[options]\n"
"#...\n"
"python_requires = >=3.6"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/dependency_management.rst:444
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"    name=\"Package-B\",\n"
"    python_requires=\">=3.6\",\n"
"    ...,\n"
")"
msgstr ""
